---
Title: Tabbing
---

# Tabbing (LaTeX's `tabbing` environment)

LaTeX's `tabbing` environment lines text up at **stops** you set as you go, the
way a typewriter's tab stops work. It is the right tool for the things a table
is too heavy for: an address list, a timetable, a few lines of pseudo-code, a
column of labels hanging in the margin. JMarkdown has all of it, in a notation
of its own with LaTeX's commands accepted alongside, and in both outputs: a
real `\begin{tabbing}` in print, and the same layout in the browser.

Write a ```` ```tabbing ```` fence. Every line is a row, and a bar plus one
character is a tab command:

```tabbing
Alice $\qquad$ |= 12 Oak Street $\qquad$ |= |kill
Name:       |> Street:          |> Phone
Alice       |> 12 Oak Street    |> 555 1234
Bob         |> 7 Elm Road       |> 555 9876
```

````markdown
```tabbing
Alice $\qquad$ |= 12 Oak Street $\qquad$ |= |kill
Name:       |> Street:          |> Phone
Alice       |> 12 Oak Street    |> 555 1234
Bob         |> 7 Elm Road       |> 555 9876
```
````

`|=` sets a stop where it stands — right after the text before it, measured as
it is drawn — and `|>` moves to the next one. So the first row here is a
**ruler**: it ends in `|kill`, which sets its stops without showing it, and it
holds each column's *widest* entry, so every row fits (the `$\qquad$` adds a
gutter, as `\qquad` does in LaTeX). Stops set from the short header words
instead would make the longer entries run under the next column. The spaces in
the source are only there to keep it readable; a run of them counts as one.

## The commands

Each mark echoes the LaTeX command it stands for, and the LaTeX command works
too:

| Mark | LaTeX | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| `\|=` | `\=` | set a stop here |
| `\|>` | `\>` | move to the next stop |
| `\|<` | `\<` | at the start of a row: back one stop, for this row only |
| `\|+` | `\+` | move the left margin in one stop, for the rows that follow |
| `\|-` | `\-` | move the left margin back out one stop |
| `\|'` | `\'` | put the text so far flush right, just before this column |
| `` \|` `` | `` \` `` | put the rest of the row flush against the right edge |
| `\|[` `\|]` | `\pushtabs` `\poptabs` | save and restore the stops |
| `\|kill` | `\kill` | end a ruler row: its stops are set, the row is not shown |
| a new line | `\\` | end the row |

`\|` is a literal bar, and marks are not read inside `$…$` maths or a code
span, so `$|x|$` is an absolute value, not two commands.

## A ruler row

A ruler is any row ending in `|kill` — it can hold placeholder text, or columns
wider than anything in the table:

```tabbing
Wednesday:   |= 10:00–11:00   |= Lecture hall |kill
Mon          |> 9:00          |> Seminar
Tue          |> 14:00         |> Lab
```

````markdown
```tabbing
Wednesday:   |= 10:00–11:00   |= Lecture hall |kill
Mon          |> 9:00          |> Seminar
Tue          |> 14:00         |> Lab
```
````

## Indenting, and stepping back

`|+` moves the left margin in by one stop for the rows that follow, `|-` moves
it back, and `|<` at the start of a row steps back one stop for that row only —
handy for pseudo-code:

```tabbing
while |= xxxx |= |kill
while there is work |+
take the next item
if it is done |+
drop it
|< else
put it back |-|-
end
```

````markdown
```tabbing
while |= xxxx |= |kill
while there is work |+
take the next item
if it is done |+
drop it
|< else
put it back |-|-
end
```
````

Here `|<` puts `else` level with its `if`, one stop out from the rows around
it, and the two `|-` on the last indented row bring `end` home.

## Flush right

`|'` puts the text so far flush right, just before the column it is in — labels
hanging in the margin — and `` |` `` puts the rest of the row against the right
edge:

```tabbing
Label: |= The text starts here |kill
|> Item |' The item's text lines up at the column
|> Longer item |' So does this one
A title |` page 7
```

````markdown
```tabbing
Label: |= The text starts here |kill
|> Item |' The item's text lines up at the column
|> Longer item |' So does this one
A title |` page 7
```
````

## Saving the stops

`|[` and `|]`, each alone on a row, save and restore the stops, so a few rows
can have columns of their own:

```tabbing
Left |= Middle |= Right
|[
A much longer first column |= x |kill
|> in the temporary column
|]
|> back in the middle
```

````markdown
```tabbing
Left |= Middle |= Right
|[
A much longer first column |= x |kill
|> in the temporary column
|]
|> back in the middle
```
````

## LaTeX, as written

LaTeX's own commands work too, inside the fence or as a `@begin(tabbing)`
environment, so tabbing pasted from a `.tex` file needs no rewriting. Since
`\=`, `\'` and `` \` `` are taken, the accents they usually write are `\a=`,
`\a'` and `` \a` `` — LaTeX's own names for them inside `tabbing`. Cells carry
any inline formatting, and maths:

@begin(tabbing)
*Symbol* \= $x^2 + y^2$ \= /the circle/ \\
*Name* \> $r$ \> radius \\
Caf\a'e \> $\bar{x}$ \> the m\a=ean
@end(tabbing)

```
@begin(tabbing)
*Symbol* \= $x^2 + y^2$ \= /the circle/ \\
*Name* \> $r$ \> radius \\
Caf\a'e \> $\bar{x}$ \> the m\a=ean
@end(tabbing)
```

A cell wider than its column runs on under the next one, as it does in LaTeX —
tabbing is for text you know will fit.

## In print

With `--to latex` a tabbing block becomes the environment itself, every mark
its command. The first example above is written as:

```latex
\begin{tabbing}
Alice $\qquad$  \= 12 Oak Street $\qquad$  \= \kill
Name: \> Street: \> Phone \\
Alice \> 12 Oak Street \> 555 1234 \\
Bob \> 7 Elm Road \> 555 9876
\end{tabbing}
```

`tabbing` is part of LaTeX itself, so no package is needed, and it compiles
under pdfLaTeX and LuaLaTeX alike. The accents go back to `\a'{e}` and its
kin, which every engine can typeset. A push or pop row is not a line of its
own: its command precedes the next row.

## On the web

A stop depends on the *rendered* width of the text before it, so on the web
the layout is done in the browser. The HTML carries the rows and pieces with
a marker for every command; a small script (`src/tabbing-page.js`) measures
each piece and places it by replaying LaTeX's own algorithm, the one in
`latex.ltx`. It lays a block out again whenever a piece changes size — a web
font arriving, MathJax typesetting a cell, the window resizing a flush-right
row — and in [watch mode](watch-mode.html) whenever a live update changes the
block.

The default template includes the script on any page with a tabbing block. A
[custom template](output-templates.html) opts in with the same section:

```
{{#Tabbing_script}}
<script>
{{{.}}}
</script>
{{/Tabbing_script}}
```

Without it — in a page with no script, or before the script has run — the
pieces sit in line with a gap at each stop: readable, though not aligned.
